# funes-julio — settings this extension reads. Append to the core's
# config/funes.local (secrets) or funes.conf (defaults); the core's loader is
# what puts them in the environment. Nothing here is read by the core itself.
#
# Where this checkout lives. agents/gmail-assistant.yaml and
# agents/whatsapp-assistant.yaml name their MCP servers as
# ${FUNES_JULIO_DIR}/third-party/... — a path that differs between a dev box and
# the deployment host, so it must not be committed literally. You do not
# normally set this: src/register.cpp publishes it at startup from the path
# extension.cmake baked into the binary, so a `git pull && cmake --build` is
# enough on either machine. Set it only to point somewhere else.
# FUNES_JULIO_DIR=/home/me/funes-julio
#
# Where the extension's own files live. Defaults are relative to this
# repository's checkout (baked in at build time by extension.cmake), so these
# are only needed to point somewhere else.
# (Spelled out: this file is read as plain key=value, ${...} is not expanded
# here. Expansion happens in agent YAML, not in funes.conf/funes.local.)
# FUNES_PUBLICATIONS_DIR=/home/me/funes-julio/publications
# FUNES_PIPELINES_DIR=/home/me/funes-julio/pipelines
# FUNES_PUBLISHING_DIR=/home/me/funes-julio/publishing
#
# Children no longer inherit the server's environment (core 5.0.1), so a
# credential publish_issue.py / send_newsletter.py needs at send time belongs
# in <publish dir>/.env, or is named in FUNES_CHILD_ENV.

# ── Email (Gmail) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by publishing/send_newsletter.py (SMTP send) and the gmail-assistant
# agent's imap-email-mcp server (IMAP search/read/draft) — same Gmail account,
# one Google App Password covers both. Generate one at:
# https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords (requires 2FA).
#   GMAIL_ADDRESS=you@gmail.com        (set in funes.local!)
#   GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=...             (set in funes.local!)
#   IMAP_USER=you@gmail.com            (set in funes.local!, same as GMAIL_ADDRESS)
#   IMAP_PASSWORD=...                  (set in funes.local!, same as GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD)
# GMAIL_* reach send_newsletter.py only through <publish dir>/.env now (or
# FUNES_CHILD_ENV=GMAIL_ADDRESS,GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD); IMAP_* reach the MCP
# server through agents/gmail-assistant.yaml's env: block, which expands them.

# ── WhatsApp ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two independent WhatsApp numbers, two independent bridge instances of the
# same binary (third-party/whatsapp-mcp/whatsapp-bridge) — see the README's
# WhatsApp section, scripts/whatsapp-bridge.service, and
# scripts/whatsapp-bridge-funes.service.
#
#   Personal number  → whatsapp-bridge.service       → port 8090, store/
#     Used by whatsapp-assistant (Funes checking/sending from your own
#     WhatsApp when you ask it to, via the whatsapp-mcp-server MCP layer —
#     its WHATSAPP_API_BASE_URL is hardcoded to 8090, no config needed here).
#   Dedicated Funes number → whatsapp-bridge-funes.service → port 8091, store-funes/
#     Used only by scripts/whatsapp_autoresponder.py (not by the funes
#     binary itself), via the two settings below.
WHATSAPP_DB_PATH=/home/me/funes-julio/third-party/whatsapp-mcp/whatsapp-bridge/store-funes/messages.db
WHATSAPP_BRIDGE_URL=http://localhost:8091

# WHATSAPP_WHITELIST is a comma-separated list of chat_jid values (NOT phone
# numbers — WhatsApp increasingly assigns opaque "@lid" ids instead of
# "<phone>@s.whatsapp.net", so matching by chat_jid is the only reliable
# option), looked up against the dedicated-number instance above. Only
# messages from chats on this list get an auto-reply; every other chat is
# silently ignored. Manage it with scripts/whatsapp_whitelist.py instead of
# hand-editing this file:
#   python3 scripts/whatsapp_whitelist.py list
#   python3 scripts/whatsapp_whitelist.py add <name-or-jid-substring>
#   python3 scripts/whatsapp_whitelist.py remove <name-or-jid-substring>
# (restart whatsapp-autoresponder.service after either command)
# Whitelisting a group chat (jid ending in @g.us) means auto-replying to
# every message anyone posts there — probably not what you want; `add`
# refuses groups unless you pass --allow-group.
#   WHATSAPP_WHITELIST=<chat_jid>,<chat_jid>   (set in funes.local!)

# Which agent answers depends on the sender's role, resolved from their jid by
# Funes (`funes jid-map <jid> <username>`). Funes has its own number, so you
# messaging your own assistant arrives looking exactly like a contact — and
# before 4.0 you got the same declawed whatsapp-autoresponder a stranger gets:
# memory and read_file, no delegation, so it correctly reported it could not
# read your email even with gmail-assistant loaded one delegation away. An
# admin now gets WHATSAPP_ADMIN_AGENT instead; every other role keeps
# whatsapp-autoresponder.
#
# This is only routing. It grants nothing: the server resolves the user from
# the jid and applies that user's own agent and tool allowlists, so pointing a
# member at a fuller agent would just be refused. Widen or narrow what an admin
# can reach over WhatsApp with `funes user permissions`, not here.
#   WHATSAPP_ADMIN_AGENT=funes
# `funes` answers at web-UI length; if that reads as an essay on a phone, point
# this at your own agent yaml with a shorter-replies prompt.

# Documents ("document" attachments — PDFs and plain text files) and photos
# ("image" attachments; audio/video are still ignored) sent by a whitelisted
# contact are downloaded and copied here so whatsapp-autoresponder's
# read_file tool can analyze them (see workspace_dir in
# agents/whatsapp-autoresponder.yaml) — images need a vision-capable LLM
# backend (--mmproj loaded) to actually be read, not just downloaded.
# Files older than WHATSAPP_UPLOAD_MAX_AGE_DAYS are deleted automatically.
#
# 4.0: uploads land inside the *sending contact's own* Funes workspace —
# $FUNES_WORKSPACE_DIR/<user_id>/whatsapp-uploads/ — because that is the only
# place that user's read_file may look. The number has to be mapped to a user
# first (`funes jid-map <jid> <username>`); an unmapped number's attachments
# are ignored, the same as its messages.
#
# WHATSAPP_UPLOAD_SUBDIR is the folder name inside each user's workspace and
# must match `workspace_dir` in agents/whatsapp-autoresponder.yaml — change
# both or neither. (It replaces the pre-4.0 WHATSAPP_UPLOAD_DIR, which named
# one shared directory for everybody.)
# Defaults shown; uncomment to override in funes.local.
# An attachment's path is handed to the agent relative to *its* workspace root:
# bare for whatsapp-autoresponder (whose workspace_dir is the upload folder),
# prefixed with WHATSAPP_UPLOAD_SUBDIR for an admin agent that declares no
# workspace_dir and is rooted one level up. Same file either way.
#   WHATSAPP_UPLOAD_SUBDIR=whatsapp-uploads
#   WHATSAPP_MAX_MEDIA_BYTES=20971520
#   WHATSAPP_UPLOAD_MAX_AGE_DAYS=30


# ── home agent: the living-room TV (scriptlib/tv_art) ─────────────────────────
# The Samsung Frame's address (reserve it in the router's static DHCP) and
# where its pairing token is kept — outside every workspace, so no agent-driven
# tool can read it. The first run shows an "Allow?" prompt on the TV; accept it
# once with the remote. Revoke in the TV's Settings → General → External
# Device Manager → Device Connection Manager.
#   FUNES_TV_HOST=192.168.1.55
#   FUNES_TV_TOKEN_FILE=/home/julio/.funes-v4/samsung_tv_token
# The Frame drops off the network when Art Mode blanks the screen; with its MAC
# set, tv_art sends Wake-on-LAN and retries. Needs the TV's "Power On with
# Mobile" (Settings → General → External Device Manager) switched on.
#   FUNES_TV_MAC=c8:12:0b:1c:91:7a
# Where the "art and weather" picture (scriptlib/tv_weather_art) gets its
# forecast from. Coordinates from Open-Meteo's geocoding; the place name is
# what the placard prints.
#   FUNES_WEATHER_LAT=-34.51236
#   FUNES_WEATHER_LON=-58.5458
#   FUNES_WEATHER_PLACE=Villa Adelina


# ── speak: the living-room Echo as a speaker (scriptlib/speak) ────────────────
# The Echo's Bluetooth *audio* address — pair it with the host first
# (bluetoothctl pair/trust/connect); the right one is the address whose
# `bluetoothctl info` lists "Audio Sink" (the other, a BLE address, is not it).
# Needs espeak-ng and PulseAudio (pulseaudio-module-bluetooth) on the host.
#   FUNES_ECHO_MAC=78:6C:84:CB:44:AF
# Hours (local time) when nothing is spoken; "off" disables. Default 23:00-07:00.
#   FUNES_SPEAK_QUIET=23:00-07:00
